WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:06.500
Peace Dividend Your government spends more on camouflage paint than on curing Alzheimer's.

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:14.700
Move one percent of the bomb budget to medicine: twenty-seven point two billion dollars for clinical trials, plus one hundred

3
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.560
and fourteen billion dollars saved by slightly fewer explosions.

4
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:24.280
The worst-case scenario is still the largest increase in medical research in history.

5
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:33.660
Here's what one percent less murder money buys you. The one percent Treaty Fund gets twenty-seven point two billion dollars

6
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:43.480
to subsidize hyper-efficient pragmatic clinical trials. The world saves one hundred and fourteen billion dollars it would

7
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:49.500
have spent rebuilding bombed hospitals, treating shrapnel wounds, and housing refugees.

8
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:59.300
Your lifetime tab for wars in countries you can't find on a map: one hundred and thirteen thousand, five hundred and fifty-one

9
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:06.840
dollars per person over eighty years. That's per person. Every person on Earth.

10
00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:16.240
Including you. You personally have been billed one hundred and thirteen thousand, five hundred and fifty-one dollars for

11
00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:22.760
explosions on the other side of the planet, and nobody sent you an invoice because they knew you'd cancel.

12
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:32.680
How one percent Less Violence Pays For Everything. An analysis of a decentralized F.D.A.

13
00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:38.940
shows pragmatic clinical trials can be forty-four point one times more efficient.

14
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:47.800
Two ways you profit from building one percent fewer bombs: The Captured Money: twenty-seven point two billion dollars per

15
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:57.320
year. Your governments spend two point seven two trillion dollars yearly on things whose sole purpose is making other things

16
00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:08.660
stop existing. Redirect one percent: Two point seven two trillion dollars multiplied by point zero one equals twenty-seven

17
00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:13.540
point two billion dollars. The rounding error on your murder budget.

18
00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:19.160
The Bonus Savings: one hundred and fourteen billion dollars per year.

19
00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:29.480
Wars cost eleven point four trillion dollars per year. Build one percent fewer bombs, fight one percent fewer wars, save

20
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:39.240
one hundred and fourteen billion dollars. Eleven point four trillion dollars multiplied by point zero one equals one hundred

21
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:48.440
and fourteen billion dollars. Money no longer spent on unblowing-up hospitals and bridges, removing shrapnel from people

22
00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:57.320
you put shrapnel into, housing refugees whose homes you converted into craters, and routing shipping containers around minefields.

23
00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:04.380
Your species has an entire industry dedicated to rebuilding things it blew up.

24
00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:09.780
You employ people to fill holes that you employed other people to make.

25
00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:14.910
On Wishonia, this is used as a children's fable about inefficiency.

26
00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:19.690
The children laugh. Then they ask if it's real. Then they stop laughing.

27
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:27.140
It replaced the previous worst children's fable, which was about a species that invented an instrument called "the recorder"

28
00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:34.350
and then made every child play it. The violence fable is considered more tragic, but only slightly.

29
00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:38.940
Where the one hundred and fourteen billion dollars Comes From.

30
00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:47.500
Your itemized receipt for violence. Direct costs are seven point six six trillion dollars per year.

31
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:56.000
This includes military budgets (two point seven two trillion dollars), infrastructure you blow up and then pay to rebuild

32
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:04.940
(one point eight eight trillion dollars), humans you break (two point four five trillion dollars), and trade routes you

33
00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:09.060
keep putting mines in (six hundred and sixteen billion dollars).

34
00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.680
Cutting one percent saves seventy-six point six billion dollars.

35
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.640
Indirect costs are three point seven trillion dollars per year.

36
00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:27.920
This is the part your accountants pretend doesn't exist, including lost economic growth from pointing engineers at missiles

37
00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:36.140
instead of medicine (two point seven two trillion dollars), and veteran healthcare, because the humans you send to war come

38
00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:45.900
back needing hospitals (two hundred billion dollars). This includes refugees, P.T.S.D., environmental damage, and lost human

39
00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:50.520
capital. Cutting one percent saves thirty-seven billion dollars.

40
00:04:51.640 --> 00:05:01.460
Total: eleven point four trillion dollars per year on war. One percent less war saves one hundred and fourteen billion dollars.

41
00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:10.340
On your planet, this is called "a good deal." On mine, it's called "so obvious that failing to do it constitutes evidence

42
00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:15.380
of a cognitive disability.". Plus cancer gets cured as a bonus.

43
00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:23.100
Almost forgot about that part. The benefits cascade beyond medicine in ways that should be obvious but apparently aren't.

44
00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:29.200
Addiction is a neurological disorder; cure the brain chemistry and the drug trade collapses.

45
00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:34.980
Most poverty is catastrophic medical costs; cure the diseases and people can work again.

46
00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:43.560
Radicalization is often untreated mental illness; you can't bomb someone into being mentally stable (you tried this, it

47
00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:48.820
didn't work, cost eight trillion dollars, created more radicals).

48
00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:53.650
Fix the biology and you fix the society. This is not a new insight.

49
00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:59.120
It's just one your species keeps forgetting because explosions are louder than pharmacology.

50
00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:08.240
The Two Numbers. The Captured Dividend (the money you definitely get): two point seven two trillion dollars in military

51
00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.880
spending times one percent equals twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year.

52
00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:18.340
That's a bank transfer. It happens because the treaty says it happens.

53
00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:27.620
No behavior change required. The Societal Dividend (money you save by being slightly less insane): one hundred and fourteen

54
00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:33.940
billion dollars per year. Depends on whether one percent fewer bombs means one percent fewer wars.

55
00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:43.560
The Elasticity Question. The elasticity parameter (e equals one point zero) asks the only interesting question here: if

56
00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:49.200
you cut military spending one percent, do war costs actually drop one percent too?

57
00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:54.470
Or do humans, being humans, find a way to keep killing each other at full price?

58
00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:02.860
At e equals zero point two five (the "humans are barely trainable" scenario), only a quarter of cuts reduce violence.

59
00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:11.560
At e equals zero point five (the "humans are somewhat rational" scenario, which is historically optimistic), half the cuts

60
00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:20.100
translate. At e equals one point zero (full match, the baseline assumption, probably generous given your track record),

61
00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:28.760
every dollar cut is a dollar of war cost saved. At e greater than one point zero, curing cancer together turns out to be

62
00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:35.740
better bonding than threatening each other with missiles, which is suspiciously wholesome and therefore the scenario I trust

63
00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:44.360
least. It doesn't matter. Even at e equals zero point two five, the Captured Dividend of twenty-seven point two billion

64
00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:50.980
dollars is treaty-mandated. That money moves regardless of whether you manage to be less violent.

65
00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:56.710
It's a bank transfer, not a prayer. The Part Where the Math Gets Embarrassing.

66
00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:02.160
The guaranteed floor is twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year.

67
00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:08.880
Direct budget transfer. No elasticity, no assumptions, no hoping humans behave.

68
00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:17.660
Just math. The best case is one hundred and fourteen billion dollars per year in total societal benefit; the range is wide

69
00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:26.200
because, again, your species. Even the pessimistic scenarios stay above roughly fifty billion dollars annually, which means

70
00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:32.000
your "worst case" is still the largest increase in medical research funding in human history.

71
00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:40.679
For context, the Captured Dividend alone would boost global medical research funding by roughly forty percent over the current

72
00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:48.940
sixty-seven point five billion dollars baseline. You've been spending more on camouflage paint than on curing Alzheimer's.

73
00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:57.040
Camouflage paint. To make things invisible. While Alzheimer's makes people invisible and you can't even be bothered to match

74
00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:00.860
the budget. What You're Assuming (and Where It Gets Shaky).

75
00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:07.030
The G.D.P. Multiplier. Spend a dollar on weapons, get sixty cents of economic value.

76
00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:09.980
Spend a dollar on healthcare, get four dollars and thirty cents back.

77
00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:16.780
Your species chose weapons. Every time. For centuries. On Wishonia, this would be considered evidence.

78
00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:22.160
On Earth, it is considered an anecdote, which is what humans call evidence when they don't want to act on it.

79
00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:28.180
The Lost Economic Growth component (two point seven two trillion dollars) already captures this difference.

80
00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:33.700
It's not double-counted, just consistently ignored. The Elasticity Assumption.

81
00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:39.160
The treaty requires multilateral cuts, so relative military balance is preserved.

82
00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:42.780
If your neighbor also cuts one percent, neither of you is weaker.

83
00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:48.080
The Societal Dividend assumes one percent less spending means one percent less war cost.

84
00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:54.720
Reality is messier. Some costs lag by years. Some wars are fueled by boredom rather than budgets.

85
00:09:55.160 --> 00:10:01.600
And military spending may occasionally deter a war, though it starts far more than it prevents (a bit like crediting your

86
00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:04.280
arsonist neighbor for occasionally calling the fire department).

87
00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:11.820
Here's why the elasticity is probably better than one point zero: military spending doesn't just respond to threats; it

88
00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:18.700
generates them. Robert Pape at the University of Chicago found that ninety-five percent of suicide terrorist attacks from

89
00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:24.880
nineteen eighty to two thousand and three were responses to foreign military occupation, not ideology.

90
00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:30.900
Half the attackers had no religious motivation at all (the leading group was atheist Marxists).

91
00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:40.040
Your eight trillion dollar War on Terror took terrorist attacks from about one thousand per year to nearly seventeen thousand

92
00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:47.980
by twenty fourteen. Bases provoke resistance, resistance justifies bigger budgets, bigger budgets fund more bases.

93
00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.900
It's a feedback loop, and feedback loops don't scale linearly.

94
00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:58.430
Cut one percent of the bases, you remove one percent of the provocation, which removes more than one percent of the terrorism,

95
00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:02.380
which removes the justification for more than one percent of next year's budget.

96
00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:09.740
The elasticity isn't one point zero because you're not just reducing spending; you're interrupting the cycle that manufactures

97
00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:14.580
the thing you're spending on. It's like discovering that your umbrella was causing the rain.

98
00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:20.040
(See the full data on occupation and terrorism.). And you've tested this at scale.

99
00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:27.980
After World War Two, America cut military spending eighty-seven point six percent (from one point four two trillion dollars

100
00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:36.080
to one hundred and seventy-six billion dollars in today's money) and got the greatest economic boom in human history: eight

101
00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:42.100
percent G.D.P. growth for a decade, home ownership doubled, the interstate highway system.

102
00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:47.340
The Cold War ended, spending dropped by half, and you accidentally invented the internet.

103
00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:53.080
Every time your species stops buying explosions, prosperity shows up uninvited.

104
00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:58.580
You're asking for one percent. You've proven you can handle eighty-seven point six percent.

105
00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:05.080
How Good Are the Sources? I'm being honest about confidence levels, which is more than your murder engineers do.

106
00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:13.060
The cost category of military spending, sourced from the Stockholm International Peace Research Institute, or S.I.P.R.I.

107
00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:21.980
(peer-reviewed), has high confidence. Infrastructure damage, sourced from Brown slash Watson Costs of War, has medium confidence.

108
00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:26.240
Trade disruption, from the World Bank, has medium confidence.

109
00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:32.480
Lost economic growth, based on S.I.P.R.I. estimates, has medium-low confidence.

110
00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:38.080
Psychological impact, from a PubMed meta-analysis, has medium confidence.

111
00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:43.280
Lost human capital, based on author estimates, has low confidence.

112
00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:53.640
The Safe Bet. For policy decisions, use the Captured Dividend of twenty-seven point two billion dollars as the reliable

113
00:12:53.640 --> 00:13:00.860
number. Money moved directly to medical research. No assumptions about whether humans will fight less.

114
00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:05.000
Just a bank transfer from the explosion account to the medicine account.

115
00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:10.480
The Societal Dividend is the upside if you manage to behave yourselves.

116
00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:14.080
I'm not holding my breath. (I don't have lungs, but the expression stands.).

117
00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:24.880
The Trajectory, Not the Snapshot. Skeptics ask whether one percent fewer bombs means exactly one percent fewer wars.

118
00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:32.920
This is the wrong question. It's like asking whether the first seed planted produced exactly one tree's worth of shade.

119
00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:42.720
The question is what happens when the seeds keep growing. Your species cut military spending eighty-seven point six percent

120
00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:47.140
after World War Two and got the greatest economic boom in history.

121
00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:55.420
That's the empirical answer. But even that misses the point, because the one percent treaty doesn't stay at one percent.

122
00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:04.760
Incentive Alignment Bonds are tradable securities held by rich and powerful people whose income scales directly with treaty

123
00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:11.660
expansion. At one percent, bondholder payouts are two point seven two billion dollars per year.

124
00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:17.580
At two percent, they double. At ten percent, they collect ten times as much.

125
00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:25.860
These aren't activists you can ignore at a dinner party. They're investors with Bloomberg terminals, lobbyists, and the

126
00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:34.060
same tools your weapons manufacturers currently use to expand military budgets, except pointed at curing diseases.

127
00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:44.280
At just one percent, the I.A.B. political incentive pool of two point seven two billion dollars per year already outguns

128
00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:51.740
your entire defense industry's lobbying budget of one hundred and twenty-seven million dollars per year by twenty-one point

129
00:14:51.740 --> 00:15:01.320
four times. Every expansion multiplies this. You've built a defense lobby in reverse, and it's twenty-one point four times

130
00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:11.820
stronger than the original on day one. Two Futures. Your species currently spends eleven point four trillion dollars per

131
00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:19.060
year on war. Over twenty years at current levels, that's two hundred and twenty-seven trillion dollars.

132
00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:27.080
This is the price tag of the current trajectory: terrorism feedback loops getting worse (Robert Pape showed bases create

133
00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:35.220
the terrorism they're built to stop), an A.I. weapons race that a planet called Moronia already ran to completion, and a

134
00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:41.520
species that spends trillions on self-destruction when the same capital could have cured disease.

135
00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:48.320
The other future: bondholders push for treaty expansion because their income depends on it.

136
00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:55.560
Each expansion weakens the terrorism cycle (fewer bases, fewer attacks, less justification for more bases).

137
00:15:56.320 --> 00:16:00.740
The A.I. gets trained on curing cancer instead of targeting civilians.

138
00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:09.320
The money compounds in the right direction. Run those numbers forward twenty years and the Treaty Path produces sixteen

139
00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:16.940
point five times the G.D.P. of the current trajectory. The full Wishonia Path, fifty-six point seven times.

140
00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:24.640
The G.D.P. trajectories chapter shows how compound interest does this to civilizations that stop wasting money.

141
00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:32.720
Years one to three: one percent treaty, twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year in research funding, and one hundred

142
00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:37.440
and fourteen billion dollars a year in war costs saved. Treaty passes.

143
00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:45.260
Bondholders start lobbying for two percent. Years four to seven: two percent treaty, fifty-four point four billion dollars

144
00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:50.630
a year in research funding, and two hundred and twenty-seven billion dollars a year in war costs saved.

145
00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:58.140
Politicians discover voters like living. Years eight to twelve: five percent treaty, one hundred and thirty-six billion

146
00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:03.620
dollars a year in research funding, and five hundred and sixty-eight billion dollars a year in war costs saved.

147
00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:03.620
Health lobby rivals defense lobby. Years thirteen to twenty: ten percent treaty, two hundred and seventy-two billion dollars

148
00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:03.980
a year in research funding, and one point one four trillion dollars a year in war costs saved.

149
00:17:04.819 --> 00:17:13.119
Defense contractors pivot to health. Over twenty years, the trajectory change is worth sixteen point three trillion dollars:

150
00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:21.520
three point one six trillion dollars redirected to curing diseases, plus one point zero due to terrorism feedback loops.

151
00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:29.320
Thirteen point two trillion dollars your species no longer spends on the industry of blowing things up and rebuilding them.

152
00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:36.120
That's at e equals one point zero (one percent spending cut equals one percent war cost reduction).

153
00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:43.940
Given that military spending generates the terrorism it's built to fight, the actual elasticity is probably better, which

154
00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:49.720
means these numbers are conservative. Moronia's politicians were rewarded for buying weapons.

155
00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:55.220
Moronia's contractors were rewarded for selling weapons. Moronia's A.I.

156
00:17:55.280 --> 00:18:00.620
read the receipts and optimized accordingly. Every budget vote was an instruction, and the A.I.

157
00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:06.120
was a very good student. The impact-adjusted bond, or I.A.B.

158
00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:12.680
ratchet flips the incentive structure using the same force that currently points it toward explosions: money.

159
00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:20.660
The difference between Moronia and the civilization that survived wasn't intelligence, resources, or goodwill.

160
00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:26.920
It was whether the richest people on the planet made more money from expanding the treaty or from letting it die.

161
00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:35.460
The one percent treaty isn't the peace dividend. It's the down payment on a future of so nice that your brain is literally

162
00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:38.240
incapable of even imagining it currently.
